{"text": "\"Loop probe\" can mean a few different things \u2014 could you clarify which you're after? Here are the most common interpretations:\n\n1. **Debugging tool** \u2014 a script that instruments a loop to detect infinite loops, measure iterations, or trace variable state. Example in Python:\n\n```python\nimport time\n\ndef probe_loop(iterable, max_iters=1_000_000, warn_every=100_000):\n    start = time.perf_counter()\n    for i, item in enumerate(iterable, 1):\n        if i > max_iters:\n            raise RuntimeError(f\"Loop exceeded {max_iters} iterations \u2014 possible infinite loop\")\n        if i % warn_every == 0:\n            print(f\"[probe] {i} iterations, {time.perf_counter() - start:.2f}s elapsed\")\n        yield item\n```\n\n2. **Network loop detection** \u2014 a probe that detects Layer 2 loops (broadcast storms) or tests loopback connectivity (e.g., ping to 127.0.0.1, or sending LLDP/factory-test frames to catch cable loops).\n\n3. **Embedded/hardware probe** \u2014 a test fixture that probes a control loop (PID tuning, sensor feedback loop) by injecting signals and measuring response.\n\n4. **Game/audio loop probe** \u2014 measuring frame time or audio buffer underruns in a main loop.\n\nLet me know which one (and what language/environment), and I'll build it out properly. If you meant something else entirely, a sentence about your goal will do.", "tokens_in": 14, "tokens_out": 468}